#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pal (also crew): how a Project Pals pal (or you) works with the rest of the team.

The team
  pal overview [--deep]             everything at once: pals, channels (what each is on), projects
                                     (git), memory, tasks, rooms, routines, approvals, what's stuck
  pal team                          the short roster
  pal feed [--all] [-n 40]          what happened since you last looked (finished, blocked, stuck…)
  pal peek <Pal[#ch]> [-n 60]       read a pal's screen and recent history, and what it's waiting on if blocked
                                     (a prompt and its options: only the owner answers those, in its tab)

Talking
  pal tell <Pal[#ch]> <text> [--now]
                                     message a pal; delivered when its turn ends (--now: right away,
                                     for a pal that's stuck mid-turn). You hear back when it finishes.
  pal ask <Pal[#ch]> <text> [--timeout 600]
                                     message a pal, wait for its answer, print it
  pal room ...                      group rooms (pal-room --help); pal invite <room> <Pal[#ch]>...

Hiring
  pal hire <Name> [--harness claude|agy|…] [--model M] [--role R] [--channel general] [--folder DIR] [--machine M] [--task T]
  pal channel <Pal> <name> [--brief T] [--category C] [--harness H] [--model M] [--worktree [--base BRANCH]]
                                     a new channel; --worktree gives it its own git branch and folder
  pal sleep [<Pal[#ch]>... | #ch | --idle] [--force] [-n]
                                     stop a pal's agent to free RAM (its tabs and room seats stay); skips working/blocked
  pal wake <Pal[#ch]|#ch>... [-n]    start a sleeping pal's agent again, resuming its conversation

Work
  pal task add "<title>" [--for Pal[#ch]] [--after T1,T2] [--body T] [--check "cmd"]
  pal task ls [--all] | ready | show T | claim T | note T "…" | block T "why" | done T ["result"]
            | assign T Pal | cancel T | reopen T
  pal routine add <Pal> "<when>" "<prompt>" [--name N] [--channel routines]
                                     when: weekdays 09:00, every 2h 10:00-20:00, mon,fri 4pm, or cron
  pal routine ls | log [-n 20] | run ID | pause ID | resume ID | rm ID
  pal routine edit ID [--name N] [--when "…"] [--prompt "…"] [--channel C]
  pal diff <Pal#ch> [--stat|--json] what a worktree channel changed
  pal land <Pal#ch> [--check "cmd"] merge a worktree channel's branch into its project (needs the owner)

Memory and skills
  pal remember "<fact>" [--user]    save a durable fact for your future sessions (--user: about the owner)
  pal memory [Pal]                  what's remembered
  pal skill ls | show NAME | save NAME --description "…" [--file F, else stdin] | archive NAME

Settings
  pal settings                      who the pals work for, head pal, bypass, and background LLM
  pal settings owner "Name" | head "Name" | bypass on|off | llm auto|claude|agy

Updates
  pal version                       what's installed
  pal update [check|apply|rollback] [--json]
                                     check GitHub for a newer release, install it, or put the previous one back

Approvals (the owner)
  pal approvals                     guarded commands waiting on the owner
  pal approve ID | pal deny ID ["note"]

"Pal" matches a pal's name or the start of it ("docs" finds "Docs Site"); without
#channel you get the one it's showing. hire, channel and invite are carried out by the Project Pals layout,
so Herdr has to be open on it. crewd (herdr-crew.service) delivers messages between turns, runs
routines and reports back; without it, messages are typed in right away.
"""
import contextlib
